# Files that must NOT be served as public assets.
#
# Astro copies public/ verbatim into the build output, so this file lands
# beside the built assets, which is where Wrangler reads it from.
#
# `wrangler deploy` REFUSES OUTRIGHT without the first two lines:
#   X [ERROR] Uploading a Pages _worker.js directory as an asset.
#     This could expose your private server-side code to the public Internet.
# Both are consumed by Cloudflare before the asset layer sees a request, so
# ignoring them costs nothing. They are listed even though this site currently
# emits neither — §6 may introduce an on-demand route, and the failure if they
# reappear unignored is a refused deploy at cut-over.
_worker.js
_routes.json

# Astro's server output. `build.server` is set to './_server/' in
# astro.config.mjs because `build.client` was flattened to the dist root, so
# the two would otherwise collide. It is empty while every route prerenders,
# but it must never be servable — if §6 introduces an on-demand route this is
# where the Worker code lands, and publishing it would expose server-side code.
_server

# @astrojs/cloudflare writes its OWN generated wrangler config into the client
# output directory. It is not used for deployment — the real configs are
# wrangler.jsonc and wrangler.prod.jsonc at the repository root — but it sits
# inside the assets directory, so unignored it is publicly fetchable at
# /wrangler.json, listing bindings and account-shaped configuration.
#
# THE DUPLICATE IN THE BUILT FILE IS EXPECTED. The adapter APPENDS its own
# entries to this file on the way into dist/client, and currently appends
# `wrangler.json` and `.dev.vars` itself — so the built .assetsignore lists
# wrangler.json twice. Kept here anyway: a duplicate ignore line costs
# nothing, and the failure mode if a future adapter version stops appending it
# is a published config, which is not worth relying on someone else's default
# to prevent.
wrangler.json
